/T (HTML \(HyperText Markup Language\) is the standard markup language used to create and structure content on the web. It forms the backbone of all web pages by defining elements such as headings, paragraphs, links, images, and other types of content. Unlike programming languages, HTML is not used for logic or computation; instead, it describes the structure and layout of a webpage using a system of tags and attributes.  An HTML document consists of a series of elements enclosed within tags. These tags tell the web browser how to display the content. For example, <h1> defines a main heading, <p> represents a paragraph, and <a> is used to create hyperlinks. HTML documents follow a basic structure that includes a <!DOCTYPE html> declaration, a <html> root element, a<head>section for metadata, and a <body> section where visible content is placed. )
/E (HTML \(HyperText Markup Language\) is the standard markup language used to create and structure content on the web. It forms the backbone of all web pages by defining elements such as headings, paragraphs, links, images, and other types of content. Unlike programming languages, HTML is not used for logic or computation; instead, it describes the structure and layout of a webpage using a system of tags and attributes.  An HTML document consists of a series of elements enclosed within tags. These tags tell the web browser how to display the content. For example, <h1> defines a main heading, <p> represents a paragraph, and <a> is used to create hyperlinks. HTML documents follow a basic structure that includes a <!DOCTYPE html> declaration, a <html> root element, a<head>section for metadata, and a <body> section where visible content is placed. )
