/T (introduces features such asarrow functions, classes, modules, and improved syntax, making code more concise and maintainable.  In your honeypot dashboard, JavaScript plays a critical role in enabling real-time updates. It can periodically fetch data from backend endpoints like /api/logs and /api/stats using fetch\(\), then dynamically update the HTML content without reloading the page. This allows SOC analysts to monitor attack activity as it happens. JavaScript also helps in formatting data, highlighting suspicious entries, and improving user experience.  Although JavaScript is primarily used in browsers, it can also run on the server side using environments like Node.js, enabling full-stack development with a single language. However, in your current setup, it is mainly used for frontend interactivity. )
