/Title (this by automatically adding the appropriate HTTP headers, such as Access-Control-Allow- Origin, to responses, allowing controlled access from specified origins.  The extension is easy to integrate and requires minimal configuration. Developers can enable CORS globally for the entire application or restrict it to specific routes. For example, a simple implementation involves importing the extension and applying it to the Flask app, after which all routes will accept cross-origin requests by default. More advanced configurations allow developers to define allowed origins, HTTP methods \(GET, POST, etc.\), headers, and credentials, providing fine-grained control over security.  Flask-CORS is particularly useful in scenarios involving REST APIs, single-page applications \(SPAs\), and microservices architectures, where different components often run on separate domains or ports. In your honeypot dashboard setup, it ensures that the frontend interface can successfully fetch data from endpoints like /api/logs and /api/stats even if they are served from  a different origin.  While Flask-CORS improves accessibility, it must be configured carefully to avoid security risks. Allowing all origins \(*\) is convenient for development but not recommended for production environments. Instead, developers should explicitly specify trusted domains to prevent unauthorized access.)
