/Title (SQLite3 is a lightweight, serverless relational database management system \(RDBMS\) used for storing and managing structured data. Unlike traditional databases such as MySQL or PostgreSQL, SQLite does not require a separate server. Instead, it operates directly on a single database file stored on disk, making it simple to use, portable, and easy to deploy.  SQLite uses standard SQL \(Structured Query Language\), allowing developers to create tables, insert data, and query information efficiently. It supports essential database features such as transactions, indexing, and constraints, ensuring data integrity and reliability. Despite its small size, SQLite is powerful enough to handle most common database operations required in applications.  One of the main advantages of SQLite3 is its minimal resource usage. It consumes very little memory and CPU, making it ideal for lightweight applications, embedded systems, and local development environments. Since the database is file-based, it can be easily copied, backed up, or moved between systems without complex configuration.  Python includes built-in support for SQLite3 through the sqlite3 module, which allows developers to interact with databases using simple commands. This tight integration makes SQLite a convenient choice for rapid development and prototyping.)
